WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:06.160
Did you ever wonder if certain foods
have a direct effect on your health?

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.160
Well, I’m here to
solve that mystery

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:11.600
since you are
the foods you eat.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.680
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.680
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:19.720
Today, we bring you new research
about prostate cancer

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:25.080
and we start with the sometimes
problematic results of PSA testing.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.400
While 64 percent of men
develop hidden prostate cancers

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.000
by their 60s,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.960
the lifetime risk of being
diagnosed with prostate cancer

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.480
is only about 11 percent,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.240
and the risk of dying from
prostate cancer, 2.5 percent,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.600
at the average age of 80.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.920
So most men develop
prostate cancer

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.440
but they die with their tumors

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.360
rather than from their tumors.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:50.200
Most men with prostate cancer
live their whole lives

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.800
never knowing they even had it.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.480
That's one of the problems
screening for it.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.040
Many prostate cancers
that are detected

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.200
may never have led to harm

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:03.200
even if they'd gone
undiscovered.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.840
Nonetheless,
not all men are so lucky.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.320
About 30,000 Americans die
each year from prostate cancer.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.200
So, should you get a PSA
prostate screening test or not?

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.280
PSA stands for
prostate specific antigen,

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.200
an enzyme secreted
by cells of the prostate

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.360
to liquefy semen
and cervical mucus

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:25.320
to facilitate fertilization.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.600
Elevated levels in the blood can
be a sign of prostate cancer,

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:31.640
which led to FDA approval
as a screening test

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.840
for the early detection
of prostate cancer in the 1990s.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.560
If it comes back high,
the test is usually repeated.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.160
If it's still high,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.240
the next step is typically

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.840
an ultrasound-guided
biopsy of the prostate

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.360
through the rectum.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.920
If there's cancer,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.160
then options include surgery,
radiation, and chemotherapy,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.440
or a delayal of treatment.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.960
However the USPSTF,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:58.560
the US Preventive Services
Task Force,

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.880
the main independent
scientific panel

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:04.280
that sets evidence-based
clinical prevention guidelines,

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.880
recommended against
routine PSA screening,

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.560
as does the American College
of Preventive Medicine,

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.560
the American Academy
of Family Physicians,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.160
and the vast majority,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.000
85 percent of professional
medical societies

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.640
in developed countries
around the world, opposed.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:23.200
In 2018, though,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.800
the USPSTF shifted from
a summary judgement against

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:31.360
to, quote, "the decision about
whether to be screened

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.280
for prostate cancer should
be an individual one," unquote,

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.600
which is more in line with the
"shared decision making" stance

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.960
of the American
Urological Association,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.840
the American College
of Physicians,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.920
and the American Cancer Society.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.640
In other words,

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.880
men should be informed
about the risks and benefits

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.400
and decide for themselves.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.200
However,
men who are on the fence

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.440
and don't express
a clear preference

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.040
in favor of screening
should not be screened,

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.840
according to the latest
USPSTF recommendations.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.880
More recently, an international
panel of experts concluded

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.280
that clinicians
need not feel obligated

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.240
to systematically bring it up,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:13.640
judging that most men would
decide to decline PSA testing,

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.520
given the clear harms and
small and uncertain benefits.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.280
That, however, is up to you.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.560
I personally declined,
but let's run the numbers.

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:27.000
Similar to the 92 percent
of women who didn't know

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.080
or overestimated the mortality
reduction from mammograms

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.440
by tenfold or more,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.720
89 percent of men vastly
overestimated the benefits

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:35.960
of prostate cancer screening

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:38.280
or simply had no idea.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.680
Most thought 50 prostate cancer
deaths could be prevented

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:43.840
out of a thousand men
regularly screened,

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.800
when in reality,
it's more like one.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.360
But doesn't even a 1-in-1,000
chance of not dying from cancer

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.040
make a few blood tests worth it?

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:56.720
The downsides are more
than inconvenience, though.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:59.360
About one in seven men
who undergo PSA screening

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.840
will test positive,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.760
yet in two-thirds of the cases,

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:04.840
the biopsy results
will be normal.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.800
So out of the thousand men
regularly screened,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.160
about 150 will have
a false alarm

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.400
and be biopsied unnecessarily,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.160
which can cause
minor complications

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.720
like pain and bloody ejaculate,

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.600
or in approximately
1 percent of cases,

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.160
more serious complications,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.920
like blood-borne infections
that require hospitalization.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.880
The greatest harm, though,
is overdiagnosis.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.600
Unnecessary biopsies
are bad enough,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:33.240
but nothing, compared to
unnecessary cancer treatment.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.560
Large-scale randomized trials
suggest that

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.720
20 to 50 percent of men
diagnosed with prostate cancer

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.960
would have never become
symptomatic in their lifetime.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.040
They never would
have been any wiser

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:47.240
had they not been screened,

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.680
but now they may be needlessly
heading to the operating table.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.680
About 3 in 1,000 men die
during or soon after

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:54.720
radical prostatectomy.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.480
That may help explain
why there appears to be

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.000
no overall mortality benefit
to prostate cancer screening.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.200
For every life that is saved,

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.800
another may be extinguished
for a cancer

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.240
they never would have even
known about.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.920
Another 50 in 1,000 men

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:14.160
end up with serious
surgical complications.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.760
Even if the surgery
goes smoothly,

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:19.400
about one in five men develop
long-term urinary incontinence

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.080
requiring the use of pads,

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.160
and most men--
two out of three--

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:26.680
will experience long-term
erectile dysfunction.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:28.920
Most men who receive
radiation therapy

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.953
also experience long-term
sexual erectile dysfunction

00:05:31.953 --> 00:05:34.840
and up to one in six experience
long-term bowel issues

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.840
such as fecal incontinence.

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:40.160
If this was saving your life,
it would be worth it,

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:41.760
but over 16 years,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.320
rather than being saved
from a prostate cancer death

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.160
by screening,

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.200
it may be 25 times more likely
that you were instead

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.760
overdiagnosed with a cancer
that wouldn't have bothered you.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.400
Yet you come away
after treatment

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.720
thinking the PSA test
saved your life.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:57.920
It's like with the mammograms.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.480
The people who are
harmed the most--

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:01.960
unnecessary cancer treatment--

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.840
feel as though
they've been helped the most.

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:07.720
In our next story,

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:12.240
we look at a novel approach
to help men change their diet

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.840
after a prostate cancer diagnosis.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:20.040
A cancer diagnosis is seen
as a teachable moment in medicine

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.000
where we can try to get
people to eat healthier,

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:26.600
but research suggests that
male cancer patients in particular

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.560
may be reluctant to
introduce dietary modifications.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.760
This has been attributed
to dietary changes

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.240
often being viewed as mimicking
“feminine” eating behaviors,

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.880
such as emphasizing an
increase in fruit and vegetables.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:43.440
Although healthy eating
might enhance long-term survival,

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.400
few men with prostate cancer
make dietary changes

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.240
to advance their
well-being.

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.600
Many of the cancer
survival trials for example,

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.880
required adherence to strict,
plant-based diets,

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.840
and though researchers tried
providing extensive nutrition

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.640
education and counseling
programs,

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:03.400
dietary adherence was
still a challenge.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:06.760
The way Dean Ornish
was apparently able to reverse

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.080
the progression of prostate
cancer with a plant-based diet

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:13.200
was home delivering prepared
meals to their door,

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:14.760
figuring men
are so lazy

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.120
they'll just eat whatever's
put in front of them.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:21.080
After all, male culture tends
to encourage men to go for

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.680
convenience food,
meat, and beer.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:27.760
Take Men’s Health
magazine, for example.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.440
Included in their
list of things

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:33.480
men should never
apologize for:

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.960
liking McDonald’s, not offering
a vegetarian alternative,

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:41.240
and laughing at people
who eat trail mix.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.960
It featured articles
with titles like,

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.120
“Vegetables Are for Girls,”

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:47.600
and sections like

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.120
“Men and Meat: There’s Only One
Kind of Flesh We Like Better,

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.560
and Even Then, She’d Better
Know How to Grill!”

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.600
To appeal to male
sensibilities,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.960
doctors are advised to use
‘body as machine’ metaphors,

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:03.800
framing men’s health in
terms of mechanical objects,

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.800
such as cars
requiring tuning.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.320
But if men are so concerned
about their masculinity and manhood,

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.520
maybe we should instead
share a bit about

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.840
what prostate cancer
treatment entails.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.600
The prostate is situated at
the base of the penis,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.080
and so when you
core it out

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:24.640
with a radical
prostatectomy,

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.320
you lose about an inch
off your penis,

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:31.360
IF... it gets
erect at all.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:34.480
Only 16% of men
undergoing the procedure

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.720
will regain their
pre-surgery level

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.320
of erectile functioning.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:42.240
Patients are typically quoted
erectile dysfunction rates

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.400
around 60% or 70%,

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:48.000
but studies have generally
considered erectile function recovery

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:50.280
as just the ability to
maintain an erection

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.560
hard enough for
penetration

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:53.040
about 50% of the time.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.400
So getting it up occasionally
is considered recovery.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:01.480
But when a surgeon tells a patient
he will recover function,

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.200
the patient probably assumes
that means the kind of function

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:06.800
they had prior
to surgery.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.840
And that only happens
16% of the time,

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:14.480
and only 4% of the time
in men over 60.

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:19.920
Only 1 in 25 get their baseline
sexual function back.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:21.800
And it’s not
just erections,

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:27.200
but other problems like OAP,
orgasm-associated pain

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:30.240
even years later,
and urinary incontinence

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:33.120
during foreplay,
stimulation, or orgasm.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.720
The vast majority of
couples overestimate

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:38.320
how much function
they’re going to recover.

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.880
Couples reported feeling
loss and grief.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.640
Having cancer
is bad enough

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.280
without the
additional losses.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:48.520
You’d think that
would be enough

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.360
to motivate men to
improve their diets,

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:54.320
but almost a fourth
of men newly diagnosed

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:56.200
with prostate cancer state
that they would prefer

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.480
to have their lives cut
short rather than living

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.360
with a diet that prohibits
beef and pork.

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.680
More men would
rather be impotent

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.800
than improve
their diet.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.800
It appears pleasures
of the flesh

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.760
may sometimes
even trump…

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.920
pleasures of the flesh.

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:16.440
In our final story today,

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:19.840
cranberries and pumpkin
seeds are put to the test

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.040
for benign prostatic hypertrophy.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.960
More than 50% of men in their 50's
and 70% or more of men in their 60's

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.520
suffer from benign prostatic
hypertrophy or BPH,

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:34.400
otherwise known
as enlarged prostate.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:37.840
This can result in burdensome
lower urinary tract symptoms

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.240
such as having to get up
frequently at night to pee.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:44.680
Current medical treatments are
clinically effective, but their efficacy

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:48.040
is compromised by side effects
and low compliance rates.

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:53.400
Symptoms include sexual dysfunction,
high-grade prostate cancer,

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:57.160
and depression. No wonder
there's poor compliance.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.280
And when medication treatment fails,
surgical procedures are considered,

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.000
such as transurethral
resection of the prostate.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.080
There has got to be a better way.

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:11.800
Population studies suggest
that low intake of animal protein

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:14.720
and high intake of fruits and
vegetables may be protective,

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.800
but not just cutting down
on any animal protein.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:22.520
Eggs and poultry seem to be the
worst, along with refined grains,

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.560
but no association was found
for red meat or dairy.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:30.480
Population studies aside, are there any
foods that have been put to the test?

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.120
There have been more than 30
randomized controlled trials

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:39.600
on the herb saw palmetto, and it's
been found to be totally useless.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.520
Evidently, cranberries were used
by Native Americans to treat

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:48.680
urinary ailments, but you don't
know until you put 'em to the test.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:52.520
Now, when they say dried cranberries,
they're not talking about those sugary,

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:57.200
oily craisins, but rather just
straight, whole cranberry powder.

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:01.400
And significant improvements were
seen in BPH symptoms, quality of life,

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.000
and all urination parameters

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:06.800
for just about a teaspoon a day
of powdered cranberries.

00:12:06.800 --> 00:12:10.280
So we know a teaspoon works,
but what about a third of a teaspoon

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:13.372
or a sixth of a teaspoon?
They also helped.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.520
So, you might as well buy it
in bulk for much cheaper

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.120
and just add it to a
smoothie or something.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.480
What about using a berry
that's a little tastier,

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:27.080
like drinking purple grape juice?
No benefit whatsoever.

00:12:27.080 --> 00:12:30.440
In a previous video, I talked
about the use of flaxseeds,

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.680
which may have a therapeutic
efficacy comparable to that

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:37.640
of commonly used drugs,
with only good side effects.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:40.200
Okay, so what about other seeds?

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.920
Pumpkin seeds have evidently
been used in folk medicine

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:46.000
as a remedy for prostate
disorders for centuries,

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:50.920
and in a petri dish, they can cut the
growth of BPH prostate cells in half.

00:12:50.920 --> 00:12:54.880
Scientists have also injected
pumpkin seed extracts into rabbits,

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.560
but what about people?

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:00.600
Pumpkin seed oil appears
to help with prostate issues.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:03.760
When pitted head-to-head
against the drug Prazosin,

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.000
it seemed to work
as well as the pill.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.560
The same when it was head-to-head
against the drug Terazosin.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:12.160
What they didn't have though
was a placebo group.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:14.880
It would have been nice to see how
well the pumpkin seed oil supplements

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:19.480
did against placebo, and hey,
in an ideal world, I'd love to also see

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.480
a group just given whole
pumpkin seeds themselves,

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:27.440
and boom: there it is. More than
a thousand men were randomized

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:31.360
to either a pumpkin seed
extract, a placebo,

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.920
or just about a tablespoon
a day of plain pumpkin seeds.

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.480
The study was funded by the drug
company that made the supplement,

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:41.880
but the supplement flopped,
it was no better than placebo,

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:45.400
but the pumpkin seeds
themselves worked.

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:49.120
The supplement appeared to reduce
symptoms, but not better than placebo.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:51.640
However, just the plain old seeds did.

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:54.840
So it wasn't just some compound
extracted from the oil.

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:59.360
And, in fact, we've since learned that
even an oil-free extract seemed to work.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.960
Bottom line, the researchers concluded,
is that pumpkin seed could be

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:06.880
recommended for BPH patients
with mild-to-moderate symptoms.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:10.600
This conclusion was echoed by the
European equivalent of the FDA.

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:14.680
Pumpkin seeds can be used for the
relief of lower urinary tract symptoms

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.120
related to an enlarged prostate
after more serious conditions

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:22.037
have been excluded
by a medical doctor.

